#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""Root-owned HardwareOne CM5 PWM fan controller.

The daemon owns the Raspberry Pi ``pwm-fan`` hwmon device while it is running.
It discovers the device and its bound thermal zone by sysfs identity, then
drives ``pwm1`` directly while the kernel's ``step_wise`` governor stays in
charge of the zone.

Coexisting with the governor, rather than displacing it via the ``user_space``
policy, is deliberate: Raspberry Pi kernels ship ``step_wise`` as the only
thermal governor, and disabling the zone outright would forfeit its critical
trip.  The kernel writes the cooling device only when a trip target changes,
so the two never fight continuously -- the poll loop re-reads the real duty
every tick and re-asserts, which bounds any kernel override to one interval.

Because the policy is therefore always ``step_wise``, writing it is a no-op and
cannot hand the fan back.  Every release path instead bounces the zone through
``_return_to_kernel`` so the thermal core re-evaluates and resumes control, or
attempts verified maximum PWM if even that fails.  The systemd unit also
invokes best-effort ``--restore-kernel`` discovery before start and after stop;
this covers a watchdog SIGKILL while the unique topology remains discoverable.

No caller-provided path reaches sysfs.  The only runtime command surface is a
root:hw1-fan Unix socket with the finite grammar::

    status
    mode auto
    mode quiet
    mode max

Each connection carries one ASCII command and receives one compact JSON line.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import dataclasses
import grp
